<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black Economic Empowerment</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1990s–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as BEE</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Black Economic Empowerment is a South African government policy, given legal form by the Broad-Based Black Economic Empowerment Act of 2003, that aims to redress the economic inequalities of apartheid. It encourages black ownership, management, employment and procurement through scorecards that affect government contracts and licences. Supporters credit it with expanding the black middle class; critics argue it has mainly enriched a politically connected elite.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-economic-empowerment.jpg" alt="Black Economic Empowerment" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Parliament of South Africa · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Broad-based_Black_Economic_Empowerment_Act,_2003.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
